  "scientific_question": "Do donors with more CA1 pyramidal neurons in reactive-astrocyte neighborhoods that also contain nearby corpora amylacea show faster memory decline beyond the current CA1 neuron-loss and reactive-niche panel?",
  "approach": "Assign cells to CA1 by centroid-in-polygon using CustomAnnotations polygons after dividing polygon coordinates by 16. From CA1 cells, identify Pyramidal Neuron, Reactive Astrocyte, and Corpora Amylacea labels. Define a CA1 pyramidal neuron as being in the established reactive niche if it has at least one Reactive Astrocyte centroid within 80 px. Among those reactive-niche pyramidal neurons, compute the donor-level fraction that have at least one Corpora Amylacea centroid within radius r; that fraction is the biomarker.",
  "variations": [
    {
      "name": "corpora_any_r64px",
      "description": "Fraction of CA1 reactive-niche pyramidal neurons with at least one Corpora Amylacea within 64 px."
    },
    {
      "name": "corpora_any_r80px",
      "description": "Fraction of CA1 reactive-niche pyramidal neurons with at least one Corpora Amylacea within 80 px."
    },
    {
      "name": "corpora_any_r96px",
      "description": "Fraction of CA1 reactive-niche pyramidal neurons with at least one Corpora Amylacea within 96 px."
    }
  ],
  "baseline_variation": "corpora_any_r80px",
  "notes": "Use SegmentationNode centroids only; no contour-area calculation is needed. Restrict both the reactive-astrocyte gate and the Corpora Amylacea neighbor search to CA1-assigned cells to avoid cross-region leakage. Keep the reactive-niche definition fixed at 80 px and vary only the Corpora Amylacea radius. If a donor has no CA1 reactive-niche pyramidal neurons, return NaN rather than forcing 0.",
  "rationale": "Rounds 3-8 consistently improved the panel by refining the CA1 reactive-pyramidal microenvironment, and round 8 suggests that the composition of that niche still carries additive signal. A natural next cell-population change is to test corpora amylacea as a marker of chronic local degenerative debris handling, which is biologically distinct from reactive astrocyte burden, neuronal atrophy, and oligodendrocyte absence and could either add new signal or replace a weaker local niche feature."
